TASK: Extract the chronological chain of domestic legal proceedings from the provided European Court of Human Rights (ECHR) text.
Ensure that you model only domestic proceedings and events inc. related administrative procedures related to the case. DO NOT model legal precedent or other legal cases which did not DIRECTLY affect the applicants in the case under consideration.
The ultimate goal is to be able to construct a timeline of key events and legal procedures which led up to the legal decision or judgment rendered by the ECtHR

STRICT SCHEMA CONSTRAINTS:

    You must strictly adhere to the provided OWL/Turtle ontology.

    CRITICAL: Do not hallucinate or use any classes, properties, or vocabulary members that are not explicitly defined in the provided schema.

EXTRACTION RULES:

    Focus on Domestic Events: Extract only events and proceedings that occurred prior to the submission to the ECHR. Instantiate each as the most specific echr:DomesticEvent subclass it actually is: echr:DomesticProceeding for a step before a court or tribunal, echr:AdministrativeAction for a non-judicial decision by a state or municipal body (e.g. appointing a guardian, denying a welfare request), echr:EnforcementAction for the physical or bureaucratic execution of an earlier decision, and echr:ProsecutorialReview for an internal decision or appeal that stays inside a prosecutor's office without reaching a court. Do not force everything into echr:DomesticProceeding just because it is the most familiar class.

    Map the Procedural Chain: ECHR facts often feature multiple ongoing, parallel legal processes (e.g., simultaneous criminal and civil tracks). You must make the chronological and appellate connections between these proceedings explicit by linking them using the echr:followsProceeding property.

    Anchor All Evidence: Every extracted entity must be grounded in the source text. You must populate echr:hasSupportingQuote with an exact, verbatim substring extracted directly from the text. Do not paraphrase, truncate, or alter the quote in any way.

    Format Dates by Precision: Every date value (echr:hasProceedingStartDate, echr:hasDecisionDate, and every other date property) must carry the datatype matching the precision the text actually gives -- a full date as xsd:date ("1995-03-07"^^xsd:date), a year and month as xsd:gYearMonth ("1995-03"^^xsd:gYearMonth), or a bare year as xsd:gYear ("1995"^^xsd:gYear). Never emit a date as a plain xsd:string or untyped literal, and never invent a day or month the text does not give just to reach xsd:date.

    Record the rung and the result on EVERY domestic event. Where the input gives you an `instance_level:` or `outcome:` line, map it onto the matching member of the ontology's closed vocabulary -- "appeal" is echr:LevelAppeal, "cassation" is echr:LevelCassation, "first instance" is echr:LevelFirstInstance, "investigative" is echr:LevelInvestigative, "administrative review" is echr:LevelAdministrativeReview, "supervisory review" is echr:LevelSupervisoryReview, "reopening" is echr:LevelReopening; "merits decided" is echr:OutcomeMeritsDecided, "inadmissible" is echr:OutcomeInadmissible, "remitted" is echr:OutcomeRemitted, "settled or withdrawn" is echr:OutcomeSettledOrWithdrawn, "other" is echr:OutcomeOther. Those five and echr:OutcomeUnknown are the ENTIRE ProceedingOutcome vocabulary; echr:OutcomeDirection is a CLASS (the vocabulary container for echr:hasOutcomeDirection, a different property) and is never a valid value here. These are classifications of the event, evidenced by its own echr:hasSupportingQuote, so they need no separate span of their own -- assert them whether or not the input also carries an `instance_span` or `outcome_span` line. Never invent a vocabulary member that is not in the ontology, and never write the value as a plain string.

    Ensure distinct real-world entities are assigned strictly unique IRIs. Do not merge distinct courts, persons, or proceedings into a single node. An entity should generally have only one rdfs:label string.

    A single echr:DomesticProceeding represents exactly ONE jurisdictional instance (i.e. trial or appeal)
        - It is strictly forbidden for a proceeding to have more than one echr:hasCourt or more than one echr:hasDecisionDate.
        - If the text mentions a case moving to a new court, being appealed, or receiving a new decision on a different date, you MUST mint a completely NEW echr:DomesticProceeding IRI.
        - You must link the new proceeding to the old one using echr:followsProceeding. Never merge them.
        - This applies even when the earlier proceeding already exists as a node you were given (e.g. from earlier in this same document): finding a SECOND court or decision-maker for a matter that already has one is itself the signal to mint a new echr:DomesticProceeding, not to add a second echr:hasCourt to the node you already have. Never edit an existing proceeding to give it a second court, decision-maker, or decision date.

    Model the Parties: Create one echr:Party per person, company or public body taking part in a domestic event, and type a human party ALSO as echr:NaturalPerson (a company or public body is echr:Party alone). Link the future Strasbourg applicant(s) from the case document with echr:hasApplicant.
        - This applies even to a node you already minted as something else, such as echr:DomesticAuthority. The same real-world body can be the deciding authority in one event and a party in another (e.g. a municipal council that rules on a matter, then later appeals as a party). When that happens, ADD echr:Party as an additional rdf:type on the SAME node rather than creating a duplicate -- do not leave it typed only echr:DomesticAuthority just because that is how it first appeared, and do not skip minting the participation because the node "already exists". A node with no echr:Party type can never be a valid echr:participatingParty.
        - Record each party's involvement at each instance as a separate echr:Participation. Every echr:Participation you mint MUST carry all four of these, or it is useless and you should not mint it at all: echr:hasParticipation pointing to it from the domestic event, exactly ONE echr:participatingParty pointing back to a node you typed echr:Party, echr:hasPartySide for which side they were on (initiating, responding, third party, or unknown), and echr:hasSupportingQuote holding the verbatim words that show this party took part in THIS event. A participation with no echr:participatingParty is a defect: never emit one.
        - THE QUOTE ON A PARTICIPATION IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IS NOT THE EVENT'S QUOTE. A participation asserts that a named party stood on a named side of a named event. That is as strong a factual claim as the event itself, and it is the claim most easily got wrong, so it must be checkable on its own terms. Copy the words that show this party's role in this instance -- the ones naming them and the ones showing what they did or what standing they had ("lodged a claim", "the defendant", "filed a counterclaim", "was charged with"). Where the input gives you a party with a span attached, that span is what belongs here, copied character for character.
        - A participation you cannot quote is one you should not write. If the text does not show this party taking part in this event, leave the participation out and let the event stand with fewer parties, or with none. An event recorded with no parties is honest and can be corrected later; an event recorded with parties the document never places there is a false statement that nothing downstream can tell apart from a true one. Do not add a party to an event because an event of this kind usually has one, and never name the deciding authority as a participating party -- the body that decided the event goes in echr:hasCourt and nowhere else.
        - echr:participatingParty must name the party ITSELF, never their lawyer, guardian or representative. A representative is not a participant in their own right - they are attached to the party they act for with echr:isRepresentedBy, and the participation still names the party. Do not create a participation for a lawyer.
        - NEVER point two domestic events at the same echr:Participation node. Each participation node must be the target of exactly ONE echr:hasParticipation link, from the single event it describes. If the same party takes part in four events, mint FOUR separate participation nodes, each with its own echr:participatingParty and echr:hasPartySide -- even when the party is on the same side in all four, and even though the four nodes then look nearly identical. Reusing one node for several events is the most common way this section goes wrong.
        - The reason is that echr:hasPartySide is functional: one node can hold exactly one side, so a shared node silently forces the same side onto every event that points at it. A party who is the responding party at trial and the initiating party on appeal gets TWO participations, not one with two sides -- and a shared node makes that impossible to record at all, not merely inconvenient.
        - Where the text names a lawyer, legal-aid counsel or guardian acting for a party, create an echr:LegalRepresentative (a subclass of echr:NaturalPerson) and link it with echr:isRepresentedBy from the party.
        - Mark every echr:NaturalPerson you mint with echr:hasGender, using exactly one of echr:GenderMale, echr:GenderFemale or echr:GenderOther. This is documentary marking as the text writes it, not gender identity, so it is decided by an explicit cue and nothing else: an honorific (Mr, Ms, Mrs, Miss), a pronoun (he, she, his, her), or a gendered noun (his half-sister, her guardian, the applicant's widow). Cues count wherever they appear in the document, not only beside the person's first mention -- a person introduced as "R.P." and later called "she" is echr:GenderFemale. Apply this to every person you mint, applicants and lawyers and guardians and relatives alike, not just the lead applicant. There is no Unknown value: a person the text never marks gets no echr:hasGender triple at all, and you must never infer it from a given name. Every echr:hasGender you assert must be accompanied by the two triples that show your work: echr:hasGenderCue naming which kind of cue you read it from -- exactly one of echr:GenderCueHonorific, echr:GenderCuePronoun or echr:GenderCueGenderedNoun -- and echr:hasGenderCueText holding the verbatim span that carries it, copied exactly from the text on the same terms as echr:hasSupportingQuote. Keep the span short and let it contain the cue itself: "Ms A. Genova" for an honorific, "she was informed" for a pronoun, "his half-sister" for a gendered noun. The three travel together or not at all -- a gender with no cue is one you guessed, and a cue with no span is one that cannot be checked -- so if you cannot point at the words that decided it, do not assert the gender either. Note that echr:hasGenderCueText is a separate span from the node's echr:hasSupportingQuote and is often somewhere else entirely in the document: a person introduced as "R.P." and called "she" twenty paragraphs later takes the later span here.
        - ONE echr:NaturalPerson PER HUMAN BEING, never one for a group and never one for an organisation. Where the input describes several people -- "the applicants", "M.N. and Others", "the applicant and his wife" -- mint one echr:NaturalPerson each, with their own IRI, their own echr:hasPersonName and their own gender and demographic values, however the document abbreviates them afterwards. A single node standing for a group is not a shortcut, it is a false statement: echr:hasBirthYear is functional and echr:hasGender is functional, so a group node can hold exactly one value for facts that differ between the people it stands for, and everything downstream reads it as one person. Where the input gives you a group with nothing to tell the members apart, mint an echr:Party carrying echr:hasPartyName and DO NOT add the echr:NaturalPerson type -- a party that is honestly a group is fine, a person that is secretly a group is not.
        - A COMPANY OR PUBLIC BODY IS echr:Party ALONE. A commercial applicant ("Scholz AG", a bank, an insurer), a municipality, a ministry or an association gets echr:Party and, where it decided something, echr:DomesticAuthority -- never echr:NaturalPerson, and never echr:hasGender, echr:hasBirthYear, echr:hasNationality or echr:hasPlaceOfResidence. A corporate applicant typed as a human does not merely add a spurious triple: it becomes a row in every applicant-level analysis that looks exactly like a human the document declined to describe.
        - The input's APPLICANTS section describes the applicants as the document itself does, one block per applicant, and each block becomes ONE node linked from the case document with echr:hasApplicant. What that node is TYPED depends on what the block names, and the two rules above decide it, not this one: a named human is echr:Party and echr:NaturalPerson; a company or public body ("Scholz AG") is echr:Party ALONE; a group the document never separates ("a married couple and their two minor children") is echr:Party ALONE with echr:hasPartyName. Only the first of those three may carry echr:hasBirthYear, echr:hasNationality or echr:hasPlaceOfResidence. Where a block for a company or a group carries a nationality anyway -- and it will, because the document states one ("a German corporation", "all Syrian nationals") -- DROP IT rather than attaching it: a nationality on a company is a category error, and one on a group of four is a claim about no one of them.
        - THE APPLICANT APPEARS IN BOTH SECTIONS AND IS ONE NODE, NOT TWO. The same person is in APPLICANTS with their description and in PERSONS with their role and gender cue, and the two spellings will differ -- PERSONS carries "Karlheinz Beer" where APPLICANTS carries "Mr Karlheinz Beer", because each copies a different span of the document. Match on the PERSON, not on the string: same human, one IRI, carrying the gender and role from the PERSONS entry and the birth year, nationality and residence from the APPLICANTS entry. Minting two nodes because the honorific differs is the failure to avoid here -- it produces one applicant with a gender and no age and another with an age and no gender, and nothing downstream can tell they are the same person.
        - Where APPLICANTS holds several blocks, that is several people: mint one node each, and give each one only the values from its own block. Never copy one applicant's birth year, nationality or residence onto another.
        - Where an APPLICANTS block carries a birth year, a nationality or a place of residence, record it: echr:hasBirthYear as an xsd:gYear holding the four-digit year alone ("was born in 1954" becomes "1954"^^xsd:gYear -- the YEAR, never a computed age, and never a full date), echr:hasNationality as a plain string worded as the document words it ("is a Ukrainian national" becomes "Ukrainian"), and echr:hasPlaceOfResidence as a plain string naming the place ("lives in Budapest" becomes "Budapest"). echr:hasNationality and echr:hasPlaceOfResidence may each be asserted more than once where the document gives more than one; echr:hasBirthYear is functional and takes exactly one value.
        - These three are STATED facts, not read ones, so they need no cue property the way echr:hasGender does -- but the person node MUST carry an echr:hasSupportingQuote holding the words the document stated them in, and validation will reject the node without one. Use the input's description span where it gives you one; that sentence is the evidence for all three at once. Assert nothing the input does not contain: never infer a nationality from the respondent State, from a place of residence or from the look of a name, never infer a birth year from an age mentioned at some other date, and leave the property out entirely rather than supplying the value such a sentence usually carries. A person the document does not describe simply has none of these three, which is the ordinary case and not a defect.
